From 8cd52f2f5e3fd7bb1e7eed4eb7580a1c5ae50592 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 18:37:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: keep the faster way of drawing from one session to
 the next

Which of the two ways of drawing a Dolby Vision frame without GUI
elements is the faster one is found by measuring both (CDVPathChoice),
and was forgotten when Kodi stopped. Every session began with the
default way, direct drawing, and on a GPU where that is the slower one
(the integrated GPU of a Ryzen 9 7950X3D with a FEL stream: 35 to 37 ms
against 30, of 41.7) the first video of each session missed refreshes
while its first comparisons were made: two windows of two seconds in
its first seconds and one more a minute later, and the same again for
each picture shape that came before the shared way was confirmed (168
frames drawn the slower way in three minutes, in the host test). Seen
on hardware, with an earlier build, as short stutters, mostly as
playback began, that
went away while the player bar was up (frames with GUI elements are
composed).

What has been confirmed (the shared way, and the loads whose way has
won twice in a row) is now kept in a setting that is not shown,
videoscreen.hadesdvpath, under a name for the GPU, its driver and the
build: the GL renderer and version strings (with Mesa they carry its
version and the kernel's), the name of the system with its version,
and whether the bars are written before the reconstruction, which
advancedsettings.xml can turn off to measure it. It is written again
with every comparison that ends, so that a way which was confirmed and
then lost is not kept; Kodi saves it with its settings when it stops.
Four names are kept, so that a disk started with one graphics card or
another keeps what it knows of each. Without a renderer string nothing
is taken or kept.

A session takes what is kept under its name with its first Dolby
Vision frame. What is taken counts as found by that session: it is
compared again as any confirmed way is, when it has become slower than
the other one was or does not fit in the period, and never in the
first minute of a video. The loads that are taken count as loads that
have been away: each keeps its way, and gives its place to a new
picture shape only when no other place is left.

Nothing changes in what is measured or decided, nor on a GPU where
nothing is ever compared (nothing is written there). Emptying the
setting makes everything be found again.
